A||||13||Cheever3000||Alt belt squealing again - normal adjustment?||||5||
Z||000000||Cheever3000||10-31-2001||08:06 PM||||Is it normal for the belt to squeal again just a day & a half after replacing the alt and the belt?  Do they just get loose and need tightening a few times?  Just now I left work to get something to eat, but it made a lot of squealing when I started the car but it finally stopped.  Two blocks away it started doing it again so I turned around & came back.  I'm 17 miles from home.  Hope it doesn't give me too much trouble when I leave in 5 hours.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||WampusCat||10-31-2001||08:12 PM||||That's what mine did.  After tightening it the second time, it hasn't squeaked anymore in about 3 weeks. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||California Kid||10-31-2001||08:18 PM||||New belts will stretch a little in the first 500 miles, some more than others. Time to break out the tools again.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||11-01-2001||12:59 AM||||I have a standing appt with the local dealer.  Every Friday, they either tighten one of my belts or replace it (depending on whether or not it's stretched to its limit or not).  It's a problem with our 2.8s since they don't have a tensioner, but seems to be worse on mine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cheever3000||11-01-2001||12:05 PM||||Got home fine last night, no noise.  This morning I attempted to tighten it.  Made a mark where the bottom bolt was before I loosened it, so I could see how much I was able to move it.  Well, I couldn't get it any tighter!  It squealed for a few seconds then stopped.  Took my kid to school, about a 5 mile round trip, with no further noise.  I may have to live with it doing that at startup.<P>Somebody needs to make some kind of a spreader tool to put between the alt and the bracket that's about 4 inches away from it.  It would be something you turn a screw and it spreads like a scissor-jack, then you tighten the alternator bolt, then remove the spreader.  As it is now, I can't seem to get anything in there to make more tension.||||reg||1
Z||000005||avengador1||11-01-2001||05:29 PM||||Don't forget if you overtighten the belt you will end up burning the bearing in the alternator. The belt should have some flex in it and make sue it is not out of aligment with the pulleys. Also make sure you tighten both bolts (pivot and lockdown).<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-01-2001||06:23 PM||||Squeeling belt? easy way to make it quiet.. run a bar of handsoap along it when its idling (be careful of your hands!!!!)<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cheever3000||11-01-2001||07:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>make sue it is not out of aligment with the pulleys. Also make sure you tighten both bolts (pivot and lockdown).<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Oh yeah! :slaps forehead: Once again I forgot to tighten the top bolt.  Argh!<P>Now, how the heck do you align it with the pulleys?  Doesn't it just bolt in one way?  I see no lateral adjustment.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||11-01-2001||07:49 PM||||Also, be aware that if your waterpump is flaky, it will dribble coolant out of the weephole, and it will run onto the pulley, and lube up the belt... Which of course, start screaming like a stuck pig. <P>Oh, and another thing... If the pulley is bent/out of true, it will screw up the belt double-time. Alignment problems can be 'cos of improper part relacements and things  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000009||tgowens||11-01-2001||11:56 PM||||Chuck, are you sure it isn't the a/c belt squealing? Usually it is that one as it is a smaller v-type belt......||||reg||1
Z||000010||banditbalz||11-02-2001||12:07 AM||||mine squeels like a stuck pig and the alternator is as far as it will go on the bracket. WTF?||||reg||1
Z||000011||rodmcneill||11-02-2001||12:45 AM||||I remember reading somewhere that the Dayco brand of belt was supposed to be a good one for the alternator.||||reg||1
